"Recent Papers by Larry Carter (https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~carter/ppbib.html)\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~carter/ppbib.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 87\nL0: # Recent paper by Larry Carter\nL1: \nL2: Notice: Copyrights to the following papers are held by the publishers. The attached files are preprints. Please, treat this material in a way consistent with the \"fair use\" provisions of appropriate copyright law.\nL3: \nL4:   * O. Beaumont, L. Carter, J. Ferrante, A. Legrand, L. Marchal, and Y. Robert, \"Centralized versus Distributed Schedulers for Multiple Bag-of-task Applications\", IEEE International Parallel and Distributed Processing Symposium (IPDPS'06).\nL5:   * B. Kreaseck, L. Carter, H. Casanova, J. Ferrante, and S. Nandy, \"Interference-Aware Scheduling\", International Journal of High Performance Computing Applications, Vol 20, No 1, (2006).\nL6: \nL7:   * X. Gao, A. Snavely, and L. Carter, 【0†\"Path Grammar Guided Trace Compression and Trace Approximation\"†www.cs.ucsd.edu】, IEEE International Symposium on High-Performance Distributed Computing (HPDC'06).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://csaws.cs.technion.ac.il/~itai/Courses/Cache/bit.pdf)\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 10; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://csaws.cs.technion.ac.il/~itai/Courses/Cache/bit.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 762\nL0@P0: Towards an Optimal Bit-Reversal Permutation Program\nL1@P0: Larry Carter and Kang Su Gatlin\nL2@P0: carter, kgatlin @cs.ucsd.edu\nL3@P0: Department of Computer Science and Engineering, UCSD\nL4@P0: 9500 Gilman Drive, La Jolla, CA, 92093-0114\nL5@P0: Abstract\nL6@P0: The speed of many computations is limited not by the\nL7@P0: number of arithmetic operations but by the time it takes to\nL8@P0: move and rearrange data in the increasingly complicated\nL9@P0: memory hierarchies of modern computers. Array trans\u0002pose and the bit-reversal permutation – trivial operations\nL10@P0: on a RAM – present non-trivial problems when designing\nL11@P0: highly-tuned scientific library functions, particular for the\nL12@P0: Fast Fourier Transform. We prove a precise bound for Ro\u0002Col, a simple pebble-type game that is relevant to imple\u0002menting these permutations. We use RoCol to give lower\nL13@P0: bounds on the amount of memory traffic in a computer with\nL14@P0: four-levels of memory (registers, cache, TLB, and memory),\nL15@P0: taking into account such “messy” features as block moves\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDestructors - The Rust Reference (https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/destructors.html)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/destructors.html\",\"pattern\":\"scope\"}); Total lines: 688\nL55:     moved = PrintOnDrop(\"Drops when moved\");\nL56:     // Drops now, but is then uninitialized.\nL57:     moved;\nL58: \nL59:     // Uninitialized does not drop.\nL60:     let uninitialized: PrintOnDrop;\nL61: \nL62:     // After a partial move, only the remaining fields are dropped.\nL63:     let mut partial_move = (PrintOnDrop(\"first\"), PrintOnDrop(\"forgotten\"));\nL64:     // Perform a partial move, leaving only `partial_move.0` initialized.\nL65:     core::mem::forget(partial_move.1);\nL66:     // When partial_move's scope ends, only the first field is dropped.\nL67:     }`\nL68: 【20†[destructors.scope]】\nL69: ## 【7†Drop scopes】\nL70: \nL71: 【21†[destructors.scope.intro]】\nL72: \nL73: Each variable or temporary is associated to a drop scope. When control flow leaves a drop scope all variables associated to that scope are dropped in reverse order of declaration (for variables) or creation (for temporaries).\nL74: \nL75: 【22†[destructors.scope.desugaring]】\nL76: \nL77: Drop scopes can be determined by replacing 【23†`for`】, 【24†`if`】, and 【25†`while`】 expressions with equivalent expressions using 【26†`match`】, 【27†`loop`】 and `break`.\nL78: 【28†[destructors.scope.operators]】\nL79: \nL80: Overloaded operators are not distinguished from built-in operators and 【29†binding modes】 are not considered.\nL81: \nL82: 【30†[destructors.scope.list]】\nL83: \nL84: Given a function, or closure, there are drop scopes for:\nL85: \nL86: 【31†[destructors.scope.function]】\nL87: \nL88:   * The entire function\nL89: \nL90: 【32†[destructors.scope.statement]】\nL91: \nL92:   * Each 【33†statement】\nL93: \nL94: 【34†[destructors.scope.expression]】\nL95: \nL96:   * Each 【35†expression】\nL97: \nL98: 【36†[destructors.scope.block]】\nL99:   * Each block, including the function body\nL100:     * In the case of a 【37†block expression】, the scope for the block and the expression are the same scope.\nL101: \nL102: 【38†[destructors.scope.match-arm]】\nL103: \nL104:   * Each arm of a `match` expression\nL105: \nL106: 【39†[destructors.scope.nesting]】\nL107: \nL108: Drop scopes are nested within one another as follows. When multiple scopes are left at once, such as when returning from a function, variables are dropped from the inside outwards.\nL109: \nL110: 【40†[destructors.scope.nesting.function]】\nL111:   * The entire function scope is the outer most scope.\nL112: \nL113: 【41†[destructors.scope.nesting.function-body]】\nL114: \nL115:   * The function body block is contained within the scope of the entire function.\nL116: \nL117: 【42†[destructors.scope.nesting.expr-statement]】\nL118: \nL119:   * The parent of the expression in an expression statement is the scope of the statement.\nL120: \nL121: 【43†[destructors.scope.nesting.let-initializer]】\nL122: \nL123:   * The parent of the initializer of a 【44†`let` statement】 is the `let` statement’s scope.\nL124: 【45†[destructors.scope.nesting.statement]】\nL125: \nL126:   * The parent of a statement scope is the scope of the block that contains the statement.\nL127: \nL128: 【46†[destructors.scope.nesting.match-guard]】\nL129: \nL130:   * The parent of the expression for a `match` guard is the scope of the arm that the guard is for.\nL131: \nL132: 【47†[destructors.scope.nesting.match-arm]】\nL133: \nL134:   * The parent of the expression after the `=>` in a `match` expression is the scope of the arm that it’s in.\nL135: \nL136: 【48†[destructors.scope.nesting.match]】\nL137:   * The parent of the arm scope is the scope of the `match` expression that it belongs to.\nL138: \nL139: 【49†[destructors.scope.nesting.other]】\nL140: \nL141:   * The parent of all other scopes is the scope of the immediately enclosing expression.\nL142: \nL143: 【50†[destructors.scope.params]】\nL144: ### 【51†Scopes of function parameters】\nL145: All function parameters are in the scope of the entire function body, so are dropped last when evaluating the function. Each actual function parameter is dropped after any bindings introduced in that parameter’s pattern.\nL146: \nL147:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL148:     fn main() {\nL149:     struct PrintOnDrop(&'static str);\nL150:     impl Drop for PrintOnDrop {\nL151:         fn drop(&mut self) {\nL152:             println!(\"drop({})\", self.0);\nL153:         }\nL154:     }\nL155:     // Drops `y`, then the second parameter, then `x`, then the first parameter\nL156:     fn patterns_in_parameters(\nL157:         (x, _): (PrintOnDrop, PrintOnDrop),\nL158:         (_, y): (PrintOnDrop, PrintOnDrop),\nL159:     ) {}\nL160: \nL161:     // drop order is 3 2 0 1\nL162:     patterns_in_parameters(\nL163:         (PrintOnDrop(\"0\"), PrintOnDrop(\"1\")),\nL164:         (PrintOnDrop(\"2\"), PrintOnDrop(\"3\")),\nL165:     );\nL166:     }`\nL167: 【52†[destructors.scope.bindings]】\nL168: ### 【53†Scopes of local variables】\nL169: \nL170: 【54†[destructors.scope.bindings.let]】\nL171: Local variables declared in a `let` statement are associated to the scope of the block that contains the `let` statement.\nL172: \nL173:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL174:     fn main() {\nL175:     struct PrintOnDrop(&'static str);\nL176:     impl Drop for PrintOnDrop {\nL177:         fn drop(&mut self) {\nL178:             println!(\"drop({})\", self.0);\nL179:         }\nL180:     }\nL181:     let declared_first = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped last in outer scope\");\nL182:     {\nL183:         let declared_in_block = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped in inner scope\");\nL184:     }\nL185:     let declared_last = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped first in outer scope\");\nL186:     }`\nL187: 【55†[destructors.scope.bindings.match-arm]】\nL188: Local variables declared in a `match` expression or pattern-matching `match` guard are associated to the arm scope of the `match` arm that they are declared in.\nL189: \nL190:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL191:     fn main() {\nL192:     #![allow(irrefutable_let_patterns)]\nL193:     struct PrintOnDrop(&'static str);\nL194:     impl Drop for PrintOnDrop {\nL195:         fn drop(&mut self) {\nL196:             println!(\"drop({})\", self.0);\nL197:         }\nL198:     }\nL199:     match PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped last in the first arm's scope\") {\nL200:         // When guard evaluation succeeds, control-flow stays in the arm and\nL201:         // values may be moved from the scrutinee into the arm's bindings,\nL202:         // causing them to be dropped in the arm's scope.\nL203:         x if let y = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped second in the first arm's scope\")\nL204:             && let z = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped first in the first arm's scope\") =>\nL205:         {\nL206:             let declared_in_block = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped in inner scope\");\nL207:             // Pattern-matching guards' bindings and temporaries are dropped in\nL208:             // reverse order, dropping each guard condition operand's bindings\nL209:             // before its temporaries. Lastly, variables bound by the arm's\nL210:             // pattern are dropped.\nL211:         }\nL212:         _ => unreachable!(),\nL213:     }\nL214: \nL215:     match PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped in the enclosing temporary scope\") {\nL216:         // When guard evaluation fails, control-flow leaves the arm scope,\nL217:         // causing bindings and temporaries from earlier pattern-matching\nL218:         // guard condition operands to be dropped. This occurs before evaluating\nL219:         // the next arm's guard or body.\nL220:         _ if let y = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped in the first arm's scope\")\nL221:             && false => unreachable!(),\nL222:         // When a guard is executed multiple times due to self-overlapping\nL223:         // or-patterns, control-flow leaves the arm scope when the guard fails\nL224:         // and re-enters the arm scope before executing the guard again.\nL225:         _ | _ if let y = PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped in the second arm's scope twice\")\nL226:             && false => unreachable!(),\nL227:         _ => {},\nL228:     }\nL229:     }`\nL230: 【56†[destructors.scope.bindings.patterns]】\nL231: \nL232: Variables in patterns are dropped in reverse order of declaration within the pattern.\nL233: \nL234:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL235:     fn main() {\nL236:     struct PrintOnDrop(&'static str);\nL237:     impl Drop for PrintOnDrop {\nL238:         fn drop(&mut self) {\nL239:             println!(\"drop({})\", self.0);\nL240:         }\nL241:     }\nL242:     let (declared_first, declared_last) = (\nL243:         PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped last\"),\nL244:         PrintOnDrop(\"Dropped first\"),\nL245:     );\nL246:     }`\nL247: \nL248: 【57†[destructors.scope.bindings.or-patterns]】\nL249: For the purpose of drop order, 【58†or-patterns】 declare bindings in the order given by the first subpattern.\nL250: \nL251:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL252:     fn main() {\nL253:     struct PrintOnDrop(&'static str);\nL254:     impl Drop for PrintOnDrop {\nL255:         fn drop(&mut self) {\nL256:             println!(\"drop({})\", self.0);\nL257:         }\nL258:     }\nL259:     // Drops `x` before `y`.\nL260:     fn or_pattern_drop_order<T>(\nL261:         (Ok([x, y]) | Err([y, x])): Result<[T; 2], [T; 2]>\nL262:     //   ^^^^^^^^^^   ^^^^^^^^^^^ This is the second subpattern.\nL263:     //   |\nL264:     //   This is the first subpattern.\nL265:     //\nL266:     //   In the first subpattern, `x` is declared before `y`. Since it is\nL267:     //   the first subpattern, that is the order used even if the second\nL268:     //   subpattern, where the bindings are declared in the opposite\nL269:     //   order, is matched.\nL270:     ) {}\nL271: \nL272:     // Here we match the first subpattern, and the drops happen according\nL273:     // to the declaration order in the first subpattern.\nL274:     or_pattern_drop_order(Ok([\nL275:         PrintOnDrop(\"Declared first, dropped last\"),\nL276:         PrintOnDrop(\"Declared last, dropped first\"),\nL277:     ]));\nL278: \nL279:     // Here we match the second subpattern, and the drops still happen\nL280:     // according to the declaration order in the first subpattern.\nL281:     or_pattern_drop_order(Err([\nL282:         PrintOnDrop(\"Declared last, dropped first\"),\nL283:         PrintOnDrop(\"Declared first, dropped last\"),\nL284:     ]));\nL285:     }`\nL286: 【59†[destructors.scope.temporary]】\nL287: ### 【60†Temporary scopes】\nL288: \nL289: 【61†[destructors.scope.temporary.intro]】\nL290: \nL291: The temporary scope of an expression is the scope that is used for the temporary variable that holds the result of that expression when used in a 【62†place context】, unless it is 【63†promoted】.\nL292: \nL293: 【64†[destructors.scope.temporary.enclosing]】\nL294: \nL295: Apart from lifetime extension, the temporary scope of an expression is the smallest scope that contains the expression and is one of the following:\nL296:   * The entire function.\nL297:   * A statement.\nL298:   * The body of an 【24†`if`】, 【25†`while`】 or 【27†`loop`】 expression.\nL299:   * The `else` block of an `if` expression.\nL300:   * The non-pattern matching condition expression of an `if` or `while` expression or a non-pattern-matching `match` 【65†guard condition operand】.\nL301:   * The pattern-matching guard, if present, and body expression for a `match` arm.\nL302:   * Each operand of a 【66†lazy boolean expression】.\nL303:   * The pattern-matching condition(s) and consequent body of 【24†`if`】 (【67†destructors.scope.temporary.edition2024】).\nL304:   * The pattern-matching condition and loop body of 【25†`while`】.\nL305:   * The entirety of the tail expression of a block (【67†destructors.scope.temporary.edition2024】).\nL306: > Note\nL307: >\nL308: > The 【68†scrutinee】 of a `match` expression is not a temporary scope, so temporaries in the scrutinee can be dropped after the `match` expression. For example, the temporary for `1` in `match 1 { ref mut z => z };` lives until the end of the statement.\nL309: \nL310: > Note\nL311: >\nL312: > The desugaring of a 【69†destructuring assignment】 restricts the temporary scope of its assigned value operand (the RHS). For details, see 【70†expr.assign.destructure.tmp-scopes】.\nL313: \nL314: 【67†[destructors.scope.temporary.edition2024]】\nL315: > 2024 Edition differences\nL316: >\nL353:         // `while let scrutinee` dropped here.\nL354:     }\nL355: \nL356:     // Dropped before the first ||\nL357:     (PrintOnDrop(\"first operand\").0 == \"\"\nL358:     // Dropped before the )\nL359:     || PrintOnDrop(\"second operand\").0 == \"\")\nL360:     // Dropped before the ;\nL361:     || PrintOnDrop(\"third operand\").0 == \"\";\nL362: \nL363:     // Scrutinee is dropped at the end of the function, before local variables\nL364:     // (because this is the tail expression of the function body block).\nL365:     match PrintOnDrop(\"Matched value in final expression\") {\nL366:         // Non-pattern-matching guards' temporaries are dropped once the\nL367:         // condition has been evaluated\nL368:         _ if PrintOnDrop(\"guard condition\").0 == \"\" => (),\nL369:         // Pattern-matching guards' temporaries are dropped when leaving the\nL370:         // arm's scope\nL371:         _ if let \"guard scrutinee\" = PrintOnDrop(\"guard scrutinee\").0 => {\nL372:             let _ = &PrintOnDrop(\"lifetime-extended temporary in inner scope\");\nL373:             // `lifetime-extended temporary in inner scope` is dropped here\nL374:         }\nL375:         // `guard scrutinee` is dropped here\nL376:         _ => (),\nL377:     }\nL378:     }`\nL379: 【71†[destructors.scope.operands]】\nL380: ### 【72†Operands】\nL381: Temporaries are also created to hold the result of operands to an expression while the other operands are evaluated. The temporaries are associated to the scope of the expression with that operand. Since the temporaries are moved from once the expression is evaluated, dropping them has no effect unless one of the operands to an expression breaks out of the expression, returns, or 【73†panics】.\nL382: \nL383:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL384:     fn main() {\nL385:     struct PrintOnDrop(&'static str);\nL386:     impl Drop for PrintOnDrop {\nL387:         fn drop(&mut self) {\nL388:             println!(\"drop({})\", self.0);\nL389:         }\nL390:     }\nL391:     loop {\nL392:         // Tuple expression doesn't finish evaluating so operands drop in reverse order\nL393:         (\nL394:             PrintOnDrop(\"Outer tuple first\"),\nL395:             PrintOnDrop(\"Outer tuple second\"),\nL396:             (\nL397:                 PrintOnDrop(\"Inner tuple first\"),\nL398:                 PrintOnDrop(\"Inner tuple second\"),\nL399:                 break,\nL400:             ),\nL401:             PrintOnDrop(\"Never created\"),\nL402:         );\nL403:     }\nL404:     }`\nL405: 【74†[destructors.scope.const-promotion]】\nL406: ### 【63†Constant promotion】\nL407: Promotion of a value expression to a `'static` slot occurs when the expression could be written in a constant and borrowed, and that borrow could be dereferenced where the expression was originally written, without changing the runtime behavior. That is, the promoted expression can be evaluated at compile-time and the resulting value does not contain 【75†interior mutability】 or 【76†destructors】 (these properties are determined based on the value where possible, e.g.\nL408: `&None` always has the type `&'static Option<_>`, as it contains nothing disallowed).\nL409: 【77†[destructors.scope.lifetime-extension]】\nL410: ### 【78†Temporary lifetime extension】\nL411: \nL412: > Note\nL413: >\nL414: > The exact rules for temporary lifetime extension are subject to change. This is describing the current behavior only.\nL415: \nL416: 【79†[destructors.scope.lifetime-extension.let]】\nL417: The temporary scopes for expressions in `let` statements are sometimes extended to the scope of the block containing the `let` statement. This is done when the usual temporary scope would be too small, based on certain syntactic rules. For example:\nL418: \nL419:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL420:     fn main() {\nL421:     let x = &mut 0;\nL422:     // Usually a temporary would be dropped by now, but the temporary for `0` lives\nL423:     // to the end of the block.\nL424:     println!(\"{}\", x);\nL425:     }`\nL426: \nL427: 【80†[destructors.scope.lifetime-extension.static]】\nL428: Lifetime extension also applies to `static` and `const` items, where it makes temporaries live until the end of the program. For example:\nL429: \nL430:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL431:     fn main() {\nL432:     const C: &Vec<i32> = &Vec::new();\nL433:     // Usually this would be a dangling reference as the `Vec` would only\nL434:     // exist inside the initializer expression of `C`, but instead the\nL435:     // borrow gets lifetime-extended so it effectively has `'static` lifetime.\nL436:     println!(\"{:?}\", C);\nL437:     }`\nL438: 【81†[destructors.scope.lifetime-extension.sub-expressions]】\nL439: \nL440: If a 【82†borrow】, 【83†dereference】, 【84†field】, or 【85†tuple indexing expression】 has an extended temporary scope, then so does its operand. If an 【86†indexing expression】 has an extended temporary scope, then the indexed expression also has an extended temporary scope.\nL441: \nL442: 【87†[destructors.scope.lifetime-extension.patterns]】\nL443: #### 【88†Extending based on patterns】\nL444: \nL445: 【89†[destructors.scope.lifetime-extension.patterns.extending]】\nL446: \nL447: An extending pattern is either:\nL448: \nL449:   * An 【90†identifier pattern】 that binds by reference or mutable reference.\nL450: \nL451:     `#![allow(unused)]\nL452:     fn main() {\nL453:     fn temp() {}\nL454:     let ref x = temp(); // Binds by reference.\nL455:     x;\nL456:     let ref mut x = temp(); // Binds by mutable reference.\nL457:     x;\nL458:     }`\nL511: 【96†[destructors.scope.lifetime-extension.exprs]】\nL512: \nL513: Tests\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.37710] zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710)\nciteturn8view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37710\",\"pattern\":\"Submitted\"}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 26 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Samarth Narang】, 【6†Alhad Daftardar】, 【7†Brandon Reagen】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models, by Samarth Narang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\